#import "../common.typ": *

= Appendix E. Glossary of HEOR and HTA Terms

This glossary consolidates every "Difficult Terminology" callout box introduced across the book's 35 chapters -- 176 terms in total -- into one alphabetical reference, each tagged with the chapter where it was first introduced and explained in full. Entries here are deliberately compressed to a sentence or two for fast lookup; the chapter tag points to where the complete explanation, analogy, and worked example (where one was given) can be found.

#v(0.5em)

#glossary-entry("\"ML\" and \"PM\" as tau-squared estimators", [Maximum-likelihood (ML) and Paule-Mandel (PM) are two numerical methods for estimating tau-squared, the between-study variance in meta-analysis; PM is an iterative estimator often recommended as an alternative to REML when the number of studies is small. In this context "ML" refers only to this statistical estimation method and has no connection to Machine Learning.], 11)
#glossary-entry("anchor-based method", [An approach to estimating a minimal clinically important difference (MCID) that compares change in a patient-reported outcome (PRO) score against an independent external indicator of whether the patient's condition genuinely changed by a meaningful amount, most commonly a patient global rating of change scale. The average PRO score change among patients reporting a small-but-real change (e.g. "a little better") becomes the estimated MCID; because the anchor is an external signal of what patients themselves consider meaningful, anchor-based estimates are generally considered the stronger form of evidence.], 33)
#glossary-entry("Approximate Bayesian Computation (ABC)", [A family of methods for Bayesian-style calibration used when the likelihood function is too complex or costly to compute explicitly, as is common for simulation models run many times over. It repeatedly draws candidate parameter values from the prior, runs the full simulation, and keeps only draws whose simulated outcome is close enough to the observed target; the surviving draws approximate the posterior distribution.], 27)
#glossary-entry("Attribute", [A piece of data an entity carries with it as it moves through a simulation model, equivalent to a patient-level covariate in microsimulation (e.g. age, disease status, priority). Attributes let later events and routing decisions depend on what has already happened to that specific entity.], 22)
#glossary-entry("audit trail", [A chronological, time-stamped log of actions taken in a system, kept so that any past event can be reconstructed later. In clinical/regulatory systems every edit is attributed to who made it and when; for a model application this is typically a growing table of who/when/what-changed/what-result rows stored in a CSV file or database.], 29)
#glossary-entry("Ayushman Bharat PM-JAY", [Ayushman Bharat Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana (PM-JAY) is India's flagship public health insurance scheme, launched in 2018, providing eligible lower-income households with cashless hospitalisation cover up to a defined annual sum per family. It matters to HTA because assessments feed into decisions on what PM-JAY's benefit package covers and at what reimbursement rate, the Indian analogue of NICE guidance for the NHS.], 31)
#glossary-entry("basic parameters (of a network)", [In a network meta-analysis, the effects of every treatment relative to one chosen reference treatment, numbering one fewer than the total number of treatments. Every other pairwise comparison in the network can be derived algebraically by subtracting one basic parameter from another, rather than being estimated separately.], 12)
#glossary-entry("best-worst scaling", [A preference-elicitation method in which respondents are shown a subset of items or attribute-levels and asked to identify the best and the worst, forcing direct comparisons rather than independent ratings. Repeating this across systematically varied subsets lets analysts statistically reconstruct a full preference ordering using logit-family models related to those used for discrete choice experiments.], 33)
#glossary-entry("bootstrapped estimate", [An estimate of the uncertainty surrounding a statistic (such as a mean), obtained by repeatedly drawing new samples of the same size from the original data with replacement, recalculating the statistic each time, and using the spread of the resulting values as the uncertainty measure. It avoids relying on a formula that assumes a particular (e.g. normal) underlying distribution.], 9)
#glossary-entry("Brant-type test", [A formal statistical test of the proportional-odds assumption in an ordinal regression model -- that a predictor's effect on the outcome is the same regardless of which cut-point of the outcome is examined. It compares coefficients estimated separately at each cut-point and produces a p-value; a small p-value indicates the assumption is shaky for at least one predictor, favouring a partial proportional-odds model or cut-point-specific effects over a single pooled odds ratio.], 16)
#glossary-entry("Budget impact analysis (BIA)", [A financial modelling exercise estimating the change in a specific payer's spending resulting from introducing, funding, or changing access to a health technology, over a short defined horizon (typically one to five years) rather than a patient lifetime. It differs from cost-effectiveness analysis, which asks whether a technology is worth it per unit of health gained over all time; the two use different methods and should not be conflated.], 23)
#glossary-entry("build cache / memoisation", [The practice of storing the result of a computational step and reusing it on a later run instead of recomputing it, as long as nothing it depends on has changed. In a reproducible analysis pipeline this lets a small downstream change (e.g. a plot label fix) re-run only the affected steps while reusing an unchanged, computationally expensive result such as a large probabilistic sensitivity analysis.], 30)
#glossary-entry("calibration target", [An external, independently measured outcome -- such as a registry-reported mortality rate or a published cure rate -- that a model should be able to reproduce when its hidden parameters are set correctly. During calibration, candidate parameter values are tried until the model's output matches the target closely enough; the target should come from a different data source than whatever was used to build the rest of the model.], 27)
#glossary-entry("Cascade of care", [A sequential framework for tracking how a population with a disease moves through the successive steps required for cure -- typically diagnosis, treatment initiation, treatment completion, and cure. Attrition can occur at any step and compounds with attrition at every prior step, so that diagnostic or treatment success alone does not guarantee population-level benefit; the framework originated in HIV care continuum modelling and is now applied across TB, hepatitis C, and other chronic infectious diseases.], 32)
#glossary-entry("Catastrophic health expenditure (CHE)", [A classification applied to a household's health spending when it exceeds a chosen fraction of household income or consumption in a given period -- a common convention is 10%, though 25% or 40% are also used depending on the policy question. The threshold is a policy convention rather than a biologically derived cut point, and the same absolute out-of-pocket amount can cross or not cross it depending on household income, which is why extended cost-effectiveness analysis tracks this measure by income quintile rather than as a single population-wide figure.], 24)
#glossary-entry("checklist-based reporting", [A reporting requirement in which a journal, funder, or agency publishes a fixed list of items that a report of a given study type must address, with authors expected to state where each item is covered, often in a companion table. It exists to prevent accidental omission of content reviewers consider essential (e.g. the discount rate used, or how uncertainty was characterised), not to guarantee the quality of the work itself.], 28)
#glossary-entry("chi-squared distribution", [A standard, well-tabulated probability distribution describing how much spread would arise from adding squared deviations if no genuine underlying pattern were present, used as the reference yardstick for whether observed variation exceeds what chance alone would produce. In meta-analysis it is the reference distribution, with k-1 degrees of freedom, for the Q-statistic used to test for heterogeneity between studies.], 11)
#glossary-entry("child document", [A smaller Quarto (.qmd) or R Markdown (.Rmd) file that is included inside a larger "parent" document at render time rather than copy-pasted into it, so each section can still be edited, tested, or rendered on its own while the parent document acts as a table of contents pulling each section in.], 28)
#glossary-entry("Cholesky decomposition", [A matrix factorisation that breaks a correlation or covariance matrix into a "square root" matrix which, when multiplied by a vector of independent random draws, produces a new vector with the desired correlation structure built in. It is the standard method for generating correlated random numbers -- such as correlated cost and utility draws in a probabilistic sensitivity analysis -- from otherwise independent inputs.], 14)
#glossary-entry("chunk", [A fenced block of executable code embedded within a narrative document, marked off from surrounding prose (e.g. by triple backticks with a language tag), that is actually run when the document is rendered, with its output -- a table, plot, or printed text -- inserted into the final document at that point. This is the core mechanism of literate programming, ensuring reported numbers stay in sync with the model that produced them.], 28)
#glossary-entry("Cohen's kappa", [A statistic measuring how much two reviewers agree with each other beyond what would be expected by chance alone, correcting for the inflated agreement that simple percent-agreement figures can show when most decisions fall into one common category. Conventional interpretive bands are: below 0.4 poor, 0.4-0.6 moderate, 0.6-0.8 substantial, and above 0.8 near-perfect agreement.], 6)
#glossary-entry("colour-blind-safe palette", [A set of colours chosen so that the categories they represent remain distinguishable for readers with common forms of colour vision deficiency, chiefly red-green confusion. Such palettes vary lightness and blue-yellow contrast as well as hue, so that categories distinguishable to most viewers by hue remain distinguishable to colour-blind viewers by brightness.], 28)
#glossary-entry("conditional treatment effect / marginal treatment effect", [On a nonlinear effect scale (such as an odds ratio or hazard ratio), a conditional treatment effect is the effect for patients who share one specific set of covariate values, read directly from a fitted regression coefficient, while a marginal treatment effect is the effect averaged across the population's actual mix of covariate values, as reported by an aggregate-data trial. These two values genuinely differ numerically on nonlinear scales even when there is no true effect modification by any covariate, which is the basis of non-collapsibility.], 13)
#glossary-entry("confirmatory factor analysis (CFA)", [A statistical technique that tests whether a pre-specified measurement structure -- which items are hypothesised to measure which latent (unmeasured) factor -- fits how respondents actually answered a questionnaire, rather than discovering such a structure from the data, which is the separate technique of exploratory factor analysis.], 33)
#glossary-entry("conjugate posterior", [In Bayesian statistics, a case where the prior distribution and the data-generating distribution are mathematically matched so that the posterior distribution comes out in the same family as the prior, obtainable via a simple formula rather than simulation methods such as MCMC. For example, a Beta(a, b) prior combined with observed data of s successes and f failures yields a Beta(a+s, b+f) posterior; the Dirichlet distribution plays the analogous conjugate role for multinomial data.], 19)
#glossary-entry("continuous integration (CI)", [The practice of automatically building and testing code every time it changes, rather than relying on a person to remember to do so. In practice, an automated process installs the required software and its dependencies, runs the test suite and package checks, and reports pass or fail, typically within minutes of a code change being pushed.], 30)
#glossary-entry("Cost-benefit analysis (CBA) vs. cost-effectiveness analysis (CEA)", [Both methods compare an intervention's incremental cost against its incremental benefit, but differ in how benefit is measured: CEA (and cost-utility analysis) measures benefit in natural health units -- QALYs, life-years, cases prevented -- yielding a cost-per-unit-of-health ratio interpretable only relative to a threshold or another intervention's ratio, whereas CBA measures both cost and benefit in money, yielding a single net benefit figure interpretable on its own. CBA requires converting health into monetary terms (e.g. via value of a statistical life or willingness-to-pay), which is itself a value judgement rather than a neutral measurement; CEA dominates formal drug/device HTA while CBA dominates transport, environmental, and occupational safety regulation.], 24)
#glossary-entry("cost-effectiveness acceptability curve (CEAC)", [A curve that plots, for each candidate willingness-to-pay threshold along the horizontal axis, the proportion of probabilistic sensitivity analysis draws for which the intervention's incremental net monetary benefit is positive at that threshold. It typically rises from near 0 at low thresholds toward 1 at high thresholds, with the exact shape depending on the joint distribution of the underlying draws.], 25)
#glossary-entry("cost-effectiveness acceptability frontier (CEAF)", [With three or more competing strategies, each pairwise comparison has its own cost-effectiveness acceptability curve (CEAC); the CEAF instead traces, across every willingness-to-pay threshold, the acceptability value of whichever strategy currently has the highest probability of being cost-effective at that threshold. It is therefore a composite upper envelope across all strategies' curves, not a single strategy's continuous curve.], 25)
#glossary-entry("Cronbach's alpha", [A number, typically between 0 and 1, summarising how closely a set of scale items move together across respondents, used as a measure of internal consistency. Values above roughly 0.70 are often treated as acceptable for group-level comparisons and above roughly 0.90 as needed for individual-level decisions, though these are context-dependent guides rather than hard thresholds; high alpha indicates items move together, not that they measure the intended construct correctly.], 33)
#glossary-entry("cross-validation (health-economic sense)", [In health-economic model validation, the comparison of one entire decision model's outputs against those of other, separately built models addressing the same decision problem, with no data-splitting or resampling involved. This differs from the statistical/machine-learning sense of cross-validation, which splits a dataset into folds to estimate out-of-sample predictive performance; which sense is meant must be inferred from context.], 27)
#glossary-entry("cure-fraction model", [A survival model that assumes a defined proportion of the population -- the cure fraction -- experiences no excess disease-related mortality risk once past some point, behaving thereafter like a disease-free reference group, while the remaining patients continue to follow a standard survival distribution. It is a structural assumption layered on top of an underlying hazard model, not a distinct distributional family in its own right.], 32)
#glossary-entry("DAG (directed acyclic graph)", [A diagram using boxes for variables and arrows for assumed cause-and-effect relationships that makes causal assumptions explicit and checkable. "Directed" means every arrow has a direction, and "acyclic" means no sequence of arrows can loop back to its starting variable; DAGs are used to distinguish confounders (common causes of both exposure and outcome, which must be adjusted for) from downstream consequences of treatment (which must not be adjusted for, since doing so can introduce bias).], 16)
#glossary-entry("degrees of freedom (pooled multiple-imputation estimates)", [Informally, a quantity reflecting how much genuinely independent information underlies an estimate, with more degrees of freedom allowing narrower confidence intervals. After Rubin's rules pool several imputed datasets, the reported degrees of freedom is deliberately a non-whole number and is often smaller than a single complete dataset of the same size would give, reflecting a penalty for the extra uncertainty from not knowing the true missing values; the more the imputed datasets disagree with each other, the greater the discount.], 7)
#glossary-entry("DESCRIPTION file", [A plain-text, machine-readable metadata file read by R itself that records an R package's name, version, title, author, and the Imports/Depends fields listing every other package it requires. R uses it to check that all required dependencies are present before installation or use.], 30)
#glossary-entry("deviance / residual deviance", [Deviance is a single number, derived from the log-likelihood, summarising how badly a fitted model disagrees with the data it was fitted to, with lower values indicating better fit. Residual deviance is the deviance remaining after fitting; comparing it between nested models indicates whether a more complex model's extra parameters earn a meaningfully better fit or add complexity without real gain. The Deviance Information Criterion (DIC) is essentially deviance with a built-in penalty for model complexity.], 12)
#glossary-entry("diagnostic odds ratio", [A single summary statistic combining sensitivity and specificity: the odds of a positive test result among diseased patients divided by the odds of a positive result among non-diseased patients. A value of 1 indicates a useless test, while higher values indicate stronger discrimination; because it blends sensitivity and specificity, it cannot distinguish tests with very different clinical trade-offs and should supplement, not replace, reporting sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratios separately.], 11)
#glossary-entry("digital twin", [In engineering, a software model of a physical object that is continuously updated with sensor data from its real-world counterpart so its predictions stay matched to that object's behaviour. In health, the term describes a simulated representation of a patient or patient population, ideally calibrated to that individual's or cohort's actual data, used to predict outcomes or test "what if" scenarios without intervening on the real patient.], 34)
#glossary-entry("discrete event simulation", [A simulation approach that advances time irregularly, jumping directly to whenever the next event (relapse, admission, death, etc.) occurs for whichever patient it affects first, rather than checking every patient at fixed, regular cycle boundaries as a Markov cohort model does. It is particularly suited to events that are rare or irregularly timed, and to modelling shared, limited resources such as hospital beds that patients queue for.], 1)
#glossary-entry("distribution-based method", [An approach to estimating a minimal clinically important difference (MCID) using only the statistical properties of the outcome scale itself, most commonly a fraction (often half) of the scale's baseline standard deviation, or the standard error of measurement derived from the instrument's reliability. These methods are quick to compute from existing trial data without additional patient input, but are purely statistical proxies that cannot distinguish scales with identical statistical variability but different true clinical meaningfulness, so they are generally treated as a fallback used when anchor-based data are unavailable.], 33)
#glossary-entry("distributional / extended cost-effectiveness analysis", [An analysis that disaggregates a standard cost-effectiveness analysis's single population-average cost-per-QALY result into costs, health gains, and sometimes financial-risk-protection effects reported separately by subgroup (e.g. income quintile, region, disease severity, or insurance status). This reveals who gains and who pays rather than only whether the average trade-off looks favourable, since a favourable average can conceal benefits concentrated in one already-advantaged group.], 1)
#glossary-entry("Distributional cost-effectiveness analysis (DCEA)", [An extension of ordinary cost-effectiveness analysis that disaggregates costs and health effects across equity-relevant subgroups -- such as socioeconomic deprivation quintiles, severity, ethnicity, age, or geography -- rather than collapsing everything into one population-wide average. It explicitly accounts for the opportunity cost of care displaced to fund the new intervention, since the groups who rely most on displaced services are not necessarily those who benefit most from the new intervention; its output is a distribution of health gain and opportunity cost by subgroup, which can be summarised with an inequality metric or combined with the overall cost-effectiveness result into an equity-weighted index, answering not just whether an intervention is good value on average but who gains, who loses, and whether it narrows or widens health inequality.], 24)
#glossary-entry("Docker image / containerization", [A Docker image is a self-contained snapshot of an operating system, software installation, and all required dependencies, built from a text recipe called a Dockerfile. Containerization is the practice of packaging software this way so it runs identically on any machine with Docker installed, avoiding discrepancies between development, test, and production environments.], 29)
#glossary-entry("dominant / dominance (health economics)", [In cost-effectiveness analysis, a strategy is said to "dominate" its comparator when it is both less costly and at least as effective, so it is preferred regardless of the willingness-to-pay threshold. A negative incremental cost-effectiveness ratio (ICER) arising from dominance must be reported together with the explicit dominance relationship rather than as a simple cost-saving-per-QALY figure, because the same negative number could instead signal the opposite situation (more costly and less effective); the quadrant of the cost-effectiveness plane should always be checked before quoting an ICER.], 28)
#glossary-entry("dossier", [The full submission package an HTA or regulatory agency requires to evaluate a health technology, comprising a main clinical and economic summary plus technical appendices such as model documentation, search strategies, statistical analysis plans, and sensitivity analysis results.], 28)
#glossary-entry("DOTS (directly observed treatment, short-course)", [A WHO-promoted TB control strategy in which a healthcare worker, volunteer, or digital tool directly observes the patient swallowing each dose of TB medication for the full treatment course, to assure adherence and prevent relapse and drug resistance. It was the organising principle of WHO TB control from the 1990s onward, though some national programmes have since adopted more flexible, patient-centred adherence-support models.], 32)
#glossary-entry("downloadHandler()", [A Shiny server-side function that generates a file and offers it to the browser as a download when triggered. It takes a `filename` argument (the name the browser should save the file as) and a `content` function that writes the file to a temporary path managed by Shiny; it is the standard mechanism for exporting files such as CSV, PNG, PDF, or Word from a running app.], 29)
#glossary-entry("Durability (waning of effect)", [How long a treatment's benefit persists once achieved; "waning of effect" refers to the possibility that patients who initially respond or are cured may later lose that benefit through relapse, loss of corrected gene expression, or regression toward the disease's natural history. This matters for curative or one-time therapies because assuming a benefit is permanent is an extrapolation decision, often made without long-term data beyond the trial's follow-up window.], 32)
#glossary-entry("endogeneity / endogenous variable", [A variable is endogenous when it is not independent of the factors driving the outcome — typically because an unmeasured factor influences both the treatment received and the patient's prognosis, so the treatment variable partly stands in for that hidden factor. A plain regression of outcome on such a variable attributes some of the hidden factor's effect to the variable itself, biasing the estimate; instrumental variables methods are designed to address this by finding a source of treatment variation uncontaminated by the same confounding.], 15)
#glossary-entry("Entity (or arrival)", [One simulated individual moving through a discrete-event simulation model — one patient, specimen, or ambulance (`simmer`'s documentation calls it an "arrival"). Unlike a microsimulation patient modeled in isolation, an entity can wait behind other entities and compete with them for a shared, finite pool of staff or equipment.], 22)
#glossary-entry("Equity weighting", [A multiplier, greater than 1 for a disadvantaged group and typically fixed at 1 for a reference group, applied to the health gain or loss that group experiences before it is summed into a decision metric, reflecting a value judgement that reducing health inequality has worth beyond the raw health gained. The weight is not an empirical estimate but a social-value judgement, derived from population surveys, formal social welfare functions, or specified as a range for sensitivity analysis; it is a tool for making such value judgements transparent and quantified, not for removing them.], 24)
#glossary-entry("Event queue", [The simulation engine's internal, time-ordered to-do list of scheduled events (e.g. arrivals, service starts, service completions), not the patients' waiting line. The engine always executes the earliest event and recomputes what new events that triggers; it is managed automatically in `simmer`, but explains why discrete-event simulation output is driven by when things happen rather than by a fixed clock tick.], 22)
#glossary-entry("EVIDEM", [EVIDEM (Evidence and Value: Impact on DEcisionMaking) is the most widely cited health-sector-specific MCDA framework, developed from around 2008 onward and piloted or used operationally by several HTA bodies. It structures weighted-sum MCDA into a *MCDA Core Model* of roughly 14-15 quantifiable, weighted and scored criteria (quality-of-evidence, disease, intervention, and economic domains), plus a separate *Contextual Tool* of roughly six qualitative considerations (ethical and system-level factors) that are deliberated rather than scored numerically, reflecting the view that some judgements cannot be reduced to a weight and a score.], 24)
#glossary-entry("Evidence Review Group (ERG)", [An independent team, usually university-based health economists and clinicians rather than NICE staff or the manufacturer, commissioned to critically check a company's submitted cost-effectiveness model and evidence before an appraisal committee decision, functioning as a structured, published peer review with a formal right of response for the company.], 31)
#glossary-entry("exchangeability", [The assumption that justifies pooling studies in a meta-analysis: once measured differences between studies have been accounted for, there is no remaining systematic reason to treat any one study's true underlying effect as different in kind from another's, so any leftover difference is treated as random noise. When studies differ in unmeasured or unadjusted ways (e.g. markedly different baseline disease severity), exchangeability is violated and pooling them risks combining effects that do not belong together.], 12)
#glossary-entry("Expected Value of Perfect Information (EVPI)", [The expected monetary value of resolving all uncertainty in a decision model's parameters before choosing between strategies, calculated as the expected gain, averaged over every possible true parameter combination, from always making the fully-informed choice instead of committing to one fixed strategy in advance given only the parameters' probability distribution (as from a PSA). It represents an upper ceiling on the value of future research on the decision, not a forecast of what any real study could deliver.], 26)
#glossary-entry("exponential family", [A technical label for a group of probability distributions (normal, binomial, Poisson, negative binomial, gamma, and others) that share a common underlying algebraic form, which is why a single general algorithm (such as R's `glm()`) can fit all of them given a specified distribution and link function, rather than needing a bespoke fitting routine for each outcome type.], 16)
#glossary-entry("face validity", [The simplest and least rigorous form of model validation, in which a domain expert judges whether a model's structure and assumptions are plausible on their face, without running any numbers. It is a necessary first filter but not sufficient on its own, since a structurally plausible model can still produce numerically wrong outputs.], 27)
#glossary-entry("flame graph", [A visualisation of where a program spent its execution time, with each horizontal bar representing one function call, its width showing total time taken, and bars stacked vertically to show the call hierarchy (a function sits above the bars for functions it called). Reading one involves scanning for the widest bars, especially near the bottom of a tall stack, which indicate deeply nested functions consuming a disproportionate share of runtime and therefore worth optimising.], 3)
#glossary-entry("forest plot", [A standard chart showing a set of effect estimates, one per study, stacked for comparison: each row shows a study's point estimate (e.g. a risk ratio) as a square or dot with a horizontal line spanning its 95% confidence interval, with rows typically ordered by date or study size. When studies are statistically pooled, a diamond at the bottom shows the combined estimate, its width reflecting the pooled interval's precision.], 6)
#glossary-entry("frailty distribution", [In survival and multi-state modelling, an unobserved, patient-specific multiplier applied to a baseline hazard or risk to represent heterogeneity not explained by the model's variables; a patient with a frailty of 1.4 experiences every relevant transition probability at 1.4 times the cohort-average rate for every simulated cycle. Drawing one frailty value per patient, rather than re-drawing noise each cycle, creates lasting high-risk and low-risk subgroups within a cohort instead of everyone drifting toward the same average.], 35)
#glossary-entry("funnel plot / Egger's test", [Methods for checking whether a meta-analysis's evidence base looks suspiciously incomplete, specifically whether small studies with unfavourable results are missing (publication bias). A funnel plot is a scatter of each study's effect size against its precision, which should look like a symmetric, upside-down funnel in the absence of bias but becomes lopsided if small unfavourable studies are missing; Egger's test is the formal statistical counterpart, regressing effect size on precision and testing whether the resulting line is significantly tilted away from zero. Neither test proves publication bias exists, since genuine differences between small and large studies can also cause asymmetry, but both are standard first-line checks before trusting a pooled estimate.], 5)
#glossary-entry("funnel-shaped hierarchical priors (Neal's funnel)", [A geometric pathology in hierarchical models where a variance parameter and the individual-level deviations it controls are mathematically tangled, producing a parameter space shaped like a funnel: wide where variance is large and deviations loosely constrained, narrowing to a tight neck where variance is small and deviations are tightly constrained. Samplers such as Stan's HMC/NUTS struggle to navigate the narrow neck without producing divergent transitions; the standard fix is a non-centered parameterisation that expresses deviations as independent, unit-scaled quantities multiplied by the variance parameter afterward.], 14)
#glossary-entry("g-estimation of structural nested models", [A method for addressing time-varying confounding that, instead of reweighting patients as in marginal structural models, directly estimates how much a patient's observed outcome would have differed at each decision point had their treatment at that point been different. It builds a "structural nested model" of equations describing how each treatment decision affects the final outcome and iteratively solves for parameter values that make adjusted, counterfactual outcomes independent of observed treatment history after accounting for measured confounders; it is more computationally involved and less commonly used than IPTW-weighted marginal structural models, but avoids the extreme-weight instability IPTW can suffer when treatment is highly predictable from confounders.], 15)
#glossary-entry("Gaussian copula", [A mathematical device for generating jointly realistic sample values for several covariates at once from aggregate data (means, SDs, and correlations), by taking each covariate's own individually-specified distribution and combining them using a correlation structure borrowed from the multivariate Normal distribution. The result respects both each covariate's own shape and the reported correlations between them, rather than mixing values from unrelated individual distributions.], 13)
#glossary-entry("generalised linear mixed model", [A regression combining fixed effects (shared, overall parameters estimated once across all groups) and random effects (group-, patient-, or site-specific deviations from that average, with the size of the deviation itself estimated from the data), where "generalised" indicates the outcome need not be continuous and normally distributed but can be modeled on a transformed scale such as the logit. Each group is treated as having its own true value drawn from a shared underlying distribution, allowing the model to borrow strength across groups.], 11)
#glossary-entry("Generalized Additive Model (GAM)", [A regression model that, unlike ordinary linear regression's single straight line, fits a flexible, smooth curve built from overlapping basis functions to follow the actual shape of the relationship between predictor and outcome, while a built-in smoothness penalty prevents it from chasing sampling noise. In R, `mgcv::gam()` is the standard implementation, with `s(x)` in the model formula specifying a smooth term for a predictor rather than a straight-line term.], 26)
#glossary-entry("generalized gamma / generalized F distributions", [Flexible families of parametric survival curves that contain several simpler, more familiar curve shapes as special cases; the generalized gamma can behave like an exponential, Weibull, or log-normal curve depending on its shape parameters, and the generalized F distribution is even more flexible, adding a further parameter. Their flexibility requires more data to estimate reliably and makes them more prone to implausible long-term extrapolations when fitted to short follow-up, so they are best used as a model-selection check rather than a default first choice.], 12)
#glossary-entry("Gibbs sampling", [An older, simpler MCMC method for drawing representative values from a posterior distribution with several unknown parameters, which updates parameters one at a time — holding all others fixed, drawing a new value for one parameter conditional on the rest, then cycling through all parameters repeatedly — so that the sequence of values converges to draws from the full joint posterior. It was long the workhorse behind BUGS and JAGS-style Bayesian software but can be slower to explore complex or strongly correlated models than Hamiltonian Monte Carlo/NUTS.], 2)
#glossary-entry("golem", [An R package, from ThinkR, for structuring a Shiny app as an R package, with app functions living in `R/`, documented with roxygen comments, and tested with `testthat`. `golem::create_golem()` scaffolds the package skeleton, and helpers such as `golem::add_module()` generate boilerplate UI/server code for a new module.], 29)
#glossary-entry("hallucination", [In the context of LLMs, the generation of a statement that is fluent, confident, and plausible-sounding but factually wrong or entirely fabricated, with no signal in the model's output distinguishing it from a correct statement. Because an LLM predicts likely-sounding text rather than verifying facts, it can invent a hazard ratio, citation, page number, or extracted value that appears exactly as confident and well-formatted as a correct one.], 34)
#glossary-entry("Hamiltonian Monte Carlo / No-U-Turn Sampler (NUTS)", [Hamiltonian Monte Carlo (HMC) is a physics-inspired algorithm for efficiently sampling from a posterior distribution in models with many parameters, simulating a "puck" sliding across the probability landscape under simulated gravity and momentum to follow smooth, informed trajectories rather than wandering in small, nearly random steps. The No-U-Turn Sampler (NUTS) is an auto-tuning extension that monitors the simulated trajectory and stops it automatically once it begins curving back on itself, removing the need to manually tune trajectory length; Stan implements HMC/NUTS, which is generally faster and more reliable than older samplers for complex Bayesian HEOR models.], 2)
#glossary-entry("hazard function / hazard rate", [The instantaneous risk of the event of interest occurring at a given moment, conditional on having survived event-free to that point — a forward-looking, momentary rate, as distinct from cumulative survival probability's backward-looking summary of the whole period so far (analogous to a speedometer versus an odometer). A rising hazard means risk is accelerating among those still at risk; a falling hazard means the remaining survivors are an increasingly resilient group; a constant hazard means risk per instant never changes regardless of elapsed survival time, as the exponential distribution assumes, and the proportional hazards assumption underlying Cox models is a claim that the ratio of two groups' hazards stays constant even as both may rise or fall over time.], 10)
#glossary-entry("hierarchical structure (\"borrowing strength\")", [A multi-level model structure used when data come in related groups (e.g. several trials or hospitals), which estimates a group-specific effect for each group while constraining those estimates to be drawn from a shared, overarching distribution also estimated by the model, rather than treating groups as fully separate or pooling them as identical. This produces "borrowing strength": a group with little data of its own is pulled toward the overall average because it is treated as one draw from the same underlying family as data-rich groups, instead of relying purely on its own sparse, noisy data.], 2)
#glossary-entry("historical control", [A comparator group drawn from data collected before, or separately from, the trial being evaluated — for example, outcomes under prior standard of care from medical records, a disease registry, or a published natural-history study. Historical controls allow a single-arm trial to be compared against something, but the comparison is only as valid as how similar the historical population is to the trial population in case-mix, era of care, and measurement, differences that are easy to overlook and hard to fully correct for statistically.], 32)
#glossary-entry("Homogeneous mixing assumption", [The simplifying assumption built into basic SIR/SEIR compartmental models that every individual in a compartment has an equal chance of contacting every individual in every other compartment, which makes the mathematics tractable by requiring only compartment counts rather than who-contacts-whom, but breaks down when real contact structure is clustered, such as transmission concentrated within households or facilities rather than spread evenly across a population.], 22)
#glossary-entry("homoscedasticity", [The assumption, built into ordinary linear regression, that the scatter of errors around the fitted line is roughly the same size across the whole range of predicted values; its opposite, heteroscedasticity, means the scatter changes depending on where you are on the scale. EQ-5D utility data violate this badly, since responses cluster tightly at the 1.0 ceiling while scatter is wider for poorer health states, which is part of why beta regression and mixture models are preferred over OLS for such outcomes.], 8)
#glossary-entry("hot-reloading", [A development convenience, present to varying degrees in `golem` and `rhino` tooling, where saving a changed source file automatically refreshes the running app in the browser without manually re-launching it.], 29)
#glossary-entry("Human capital approach", [An older health-valuation approach, borrowed from labour economics, that approximates a person's economic worth by their expected lifetime earnings, so health is valued by the earning capacity it protects or restores. It is straightforward to compute from wage and life-table data but has a severe equity problem, systematically valuing the health of children, retirees, unpaid caregivers, and anyone outside paid employment at zero or near-zero, and valuing a high earner's health more than an identical gain in a low earner's; this flaw is the main reason the willingness-to-pay approach displaced it as the preferred method.], 24)
#glossary-entry("ICH E9(R1) estimand strategies + intercurrent events", [Intercurrent events are mid-study complications — patients dropping out, switching to rescue medication, or dying before planned follow-up ends — that complicate the definition of a treatment effect. ICH E9(R1) is the international regulatory guideline formalising five strategies for defining a treatment-effect question in their presence: *treatment-policy* (counts whatever happens regardless of adherence or switching), *hypothetical* (asks what would have happened had the intercurrent event not occurred), *composite* (folds the intercurrent event into the outcome definition), *while-on-treatment* (considers only the period before the intercurrent event), and *principal stratum* (restricts to the subgroup that would not have experienced the intercurrent event under either treatment). None is universally correct, since they answer genuinely different clinical questions, and the strategy should be chosen deliberately and stated upfront rather than implicitly by whichever analysis method is convenient.], 15)
#glossary-entry("ICH E9(R1) intercurrent-event strategies (treatment-policy, hypothetical, composite, while-on-treatment, principal-stratum)", [An intercurrent event is anything occurring after randomisation that complicates the question of whether a treatment worked, such as stopping study drug, taking rescue medication, switching arms, or dying before the outcome can be measured. The ICH E9(R1) addendum requires a trial protocol to state explicitly which version of "treatment effect" it targets in the presence of such events, offering five strategies: *treatment-policy* (counts outcomes regardless of adherence or switching, closest to real-world effectiveness); *hypothetical* (estimates the outcome in a hypothetical world where the intercurrent event never occurred, requiring an assumption since that world is unobserved); *composite* (folds the intercurrent event into the definition of a bad outcome); *while-on-treatment* (considers only the period the patient was actually on assigned treatment); and *principal-stratum* (restricts to a subgroup defined by what would have happened under both treatments, the most statistically demanding since that subgroup cannot be directly observed).], 7)
#glossary-entry("immature survival data", [Survival data in which a substantial fraction of trial patients have not yet died or progressed at the time of the data cut used for an HTA submission, so the Kaplan-Meier curve has not flattened out or reached its tail and the shape of that tail must be estimated rather than observed.], 32)
#glossary-entry("input validation", [The practice of checking that a value entered by a user is sensible before using it in a calculation — for example, rejecting a negative discount rate or a utility value above 1. Without it, a model may run without error but produce a meaningless result, such as a negative QALY gain or an ICER of zero, which is more dangerous than an obvious crash because a reviewer may not notice.], 29)
#glossary-entry("Item Response Theory (IRT)", [A family of statistical models describing the probability of a particular response to a particular item as a function of the respondent's underlying trait level and properties of the item itself, most commonly its difficulty (where on the trait scale it is most informative) and discrimination (how sharply it distinguishes respondents above and below that point). Unlike classical test theory, which produces one reliability number for a whole scale, IRT estimates parameters per item, which identifies which items are most informative and, because item parameters share a common scale, enables computer-adaptive testing, where a computer selects each patient's next question based on prior answers to shorten the questionnaire without losing measurement precision; the PROMIS instrument system is a widely used example.], 33)
#glossary-entry("LaTeX", [A typesetting system, more specialised and older than Word, that produces precisely laid-out documents and is the engine Pandoc uses under the hood when Quarto or R Markdown render to PDF. PDF rendering via Pandoc requires a working LaTeX installation on the rendering machine, separate from the R or Quarto software itself; a full LaTeX distribution (TeX Live, MiKTeX) is large, so a slimmed-down distribution is typically installed instead.], 28)
#glossary-entry("law of large numbers", [The principle that averaging the results of a random process over many repetitions brings that average closer to the true underlying expected value, even though any single repetition is unpredictable. In microsimulation, simulating a single patient is dominated by individual random noise, but averaging outcomes over many simulated patients under fixed parameters converges tightly on the model's true expected value for those parameters, which is why insufficient simulated patients (fixable by simulating more) is a distinct problem from genuine parameter uncertainty (not fixable by simulating more patients, since it arises from not knowing the true parameter values).], 21)
#glossary-entry("law of total variance (posterior variance reduction)", [A formalisation of how much collecting more data is expected to narrow current parameter uncertainty, before the data are even seen: total variance equals the expected posterior variance remaining after a study of given size, averaged over how the study could turn out, plus the variance of how much the central estimate itself is expected to move. Because total variance is fixed and already known, moment-matching methods estimate how much the central estimate would move directly from an existing PSA sample and back out the expected remaining posterior variance for a study of a given size by subtraction, without simulating hypothetical patients.], 26)
#glossary-entry("leverage points", [A data value that sits far enough outside the rest of the sample that it disproportionately pulls a summary statistic, most commonly a mean, toward itself simply due to its extremity or isolation, since the mean (unlike the median) is sensitive to the exact magnitude of every value rather than just its rank. This matters acutely for cost data because, unlike a bounded utility score, cost has no upper bound, so a single catastrophic case can be arbitrarily large and substantially distort a reported average cost and any ICER built on it.], 9)
#glossary-entry("living systematic review", [A systematic review maintained as an ongoing process, with new studies continuously or regularly searched for, screened, and incorporated into the synthesis, rather than being redone from scratch only every few years.], 34)
#glossary-entry("log-likelihood", [A single number summarising how probable the observed data would have been if the fitted model's parameters were exactly correct; higher (less negative) values indicate a better-fitting model, and it is the quantity underlying model-comparison tools such as AIC and BIC. It only evaluates fit to data actually observed and says nothing about the trustworthiness of any extrapolated portion of a model.], 10)
#glossary-entry("logit link (in beta regression)", [A link function that transforms a value confined to (0, 1), such as a probability or rescaled utility, onto an unbounded scale via $log(p/(1-p))$, allowing ordinary regression to operate freely while the inverse transformation guarantees predictions stay within (0, 1). It is the same transformation used in logistic regression, but applied to a continuous utility mean rather than a binary outcome, meaning resulting coefficients describe proportional movement toward or away from full health rather than a fixed number of utility points per unit of the predictor.], 8)
#glossary-entry("Mantel-Haenszel (MH) method", [A method for pooling 2x2 tables from several studies into a single risk ratio or odds ratio by working directly from raw event counts, weighting each study by the amount of statistical information (events) it contributes rather than averaging each study's own ratio. It is a fixed-effect pooling method, assuming every study estimates the same true effect, and handles studies with small numbers of events more gracefully than methods that first compute and then average per-study ratios.], 11)
#glossary-entry("marginal structural models", [A causal modelling approach that estimates the average outcome across a population under a specified treatment strategy sustained over time, used when time-varying confounders are also affected by prior treatment (treatment-confounder feedback), which can bias models that simply adjust for the confounder directly. It instead reweights each patient at each time point using inverse-probability-of-treatment weights so that treatment at each visit appears as good as randomly assigned with respect to confounders measured up to that point.], 15)
#glossary-entry("Market share (in a BIA context)", [In a budget impact analysis, the proportion of the eligible population currently receiving treatment in a given year that is allocated to each named treatment option (existing therapies, best supportive care, or the new intervention). It matters because the new intervention's cost to the payer is not simply the number of patients on it times its price, but that amount minus whatever cost the payer would already have incurred for patients who would otherwise have used an existing therapy, since budget impact reflects the net change in spending rather than the gross cost of the new option.], 23)
#glossary-entry("microsimulation", [An economic modelling approach that tracks many individual simulated patients one at a time, each with randomly drawn characteristics and a randomly drawn sequence of events, rather than a single average cohort moving through health states together. Averaging enough individual patients recovers the same population-level result a cohort model would give, while additionally capturing patient-to-patient variability and allowing a patient's own history to affect their future risk.], 1)
#glossary-entry("Minimal Clinically Important Difference (MCID)", [The smallest change in a score, on a PRO instrument or other clinical outcome measure, that patients or clinicians would recognise as meaningful, as distinct from a change that is merely statistically detectable; the terms MCID and MID (minimal important difference) are used largely interchangeably in the literature. Statistical significance reflects confidence that a true change is not exactly zero and can arise from a tiny, clinically irrelevant change given a large enough sample, while clinical importance reflects the size of the change itself, so a result can be statistically significant but clinically trivial, or clinically important but not statistically significant.], 33)
#glossary-entry("mixed logit model", [Also called a random-parameters logit, a model that relaxes the standard conditional logit's assumption that all respondents share the same preferences by estimating a distribution of coefficients across the population, a mean effect plus a measure of between-person variation, rather than a single coefficient per attribute. This allows it to capture not just an average preference but how much that preference varies across individuals, which is useful for understanding subgroup heterogeneity.], 33)
#glossary-entry("mixture cure model", [A survival model in which overall survival is a weighted combination of an "immune"/cured fraction, whose future mortality follows background population rates, and an "uncured" fraction, whose mortality follows a chosen parametric distribution (e.g. Weibull); the cure fraction is itself an estimated model parameter that can depend on treatment arm or covariates. This contrasts with non-mixture cure models, which instead rescale a single survival distribution to asymptote toward the cure proportion over time without separating patients into two latent groups.], 32)
#glossary-entry("model averaging", [A method that, unlike simple scenario analysis which reports each model structure's results side by side, assigns each structure an explicit plausibility weight and combines the structures' results into a single number using those weights, producing one answer that already reflects relative trust in each structure rather than several answers for the reader to choose among. This is a stronger and more assumption-laden, contested claim than plain scenario reporting, so the two should not be conflated.], 25)
#glossary-entry("Monte Carlo error", [The uncertainty introduced by the simulation process itself in a probabilistic sensitivity analysis, separate from and additional to the genuine clinical/economic parameter uncertainty being characterised; because each PSA iteration is a random draw, re-running the same PSA with a different random seed produces slightly different summary statistics (mean ICER, CEAC curve, etc.) purely by chance. Like ordinary sampling error, it shrinks as the number of iterations increases but never reaches exactly zero at any finite number of iterations.], 25)
#glossary-entry("Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse / Laplacian-type matrix", [The Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse is a generalised matrix "division" that produces a single best (least-squares) answer for systems, such as treatment networks with indirect evidence routes, where an ordinary matrix inverse does not exist because more than one algebraically valid way of distributing information between treatments is possible. The "Laplacian-type matrix" describing such a network uses the same mathematics as analysing an electrical circuit's resistor network, with treatments as nodes, trials as connecting wires, and trial imprecision corresponding to resistance, which is why solving for treatment effects in a network meta-analysis is sometimes called the "electrical network" approach.], 12)
#glossary-entry("MTEP/DAP", [MTEP (Medical Technologies Evaluation Programme) and DAP (Diagnostics Assessment Programme) are NICE evaluation routes specifically for devices and diagnostic tests, distinct from the drug-focused Technology Appraisal and the Highly Specialised Technologies programme. MTEP uses cost-minimisation for technologies expected to be cost-saving or cost-neutral, while DAP applies full cost-effectiveness modelling for technologies expected to raise net cost in exchange for better outcomes.], 32)
#glossary-entry("multi-state model", [A model that generalises both a simple survival model (which tracks only the single transition from alive to dead) and a basic Markov model (which tracks a small, fixed set of health states), by formally tracking a patient moving between any number of defined health states over time. It estimates both the probability of each possible transition and the timing of when it occurs, using survival-analysis hazard functions applied separately to each possible transition in the disease pathway.], 2)
#glossary-entry("multivariate normal distribution", [The extension of a univariate normal distribution, which describes the spread of a single uncertain quantity via one standard deviation, to several uncertain quantities sampled jointly, governed by a full covariance matrix whose diagonal gives each quantity's own variance and whose off-diagonal entries give the covariances between pairs of quantities. Sampling from it produces a vector of correlated draws in one step, which is needed whenever a relative-effect estimate across several outcomes comes with a reported covariance matrix, or whenever two or more PSA parameters are correlated rather than independent.], 25)
#glossary-entry("NAMESPACE", [A file, usually auto-generated rather than hand-edited, that tells R exactly which functions in a package are exported for a user to call versus which are purely internal helpers. It prevents an internal helper function from silently clashing with a same-named function from a different loaded package.], 30)
#glossary-entry("namespacing", [A mechanism that prefixes every input/output ID inside a module with a unique identifier so that two copies of the same module on one page do not collide by both trying to use the same ID. It is conceptually the same problem a SQL table alias solves when joining a table to itself.], 29)
#glossary-entry("Nelder-Mead optimization", [A numerical search algorithm for finding the input value that minimizes or maximizes a function without requiring calculus derivatives, by maintaining a small set of trial points (a "simplex") and iteratively replacing the worst-performing point with a better one, reflecting, expanding, or shrinking the simplex as it proceeds. It converges more slowly than gradient-based methods but does not require the function to be differentiable, which matters for model outputs, such as those from discrete Markov cycles, that are not smooth analytic functions of their inputs.], 27)
#glossary-entry("net monetary benefit (NMB)", [A single number converting a cost-and-QALY pair by multiplying QALYs by a willingness-to-pay threshold $lambda$ and subtracting cost: $"NMB" = lambda times "QALYs" - "Cost"$; the incremental version, $Delta"NMB" = lambda times Delta"QALY" - Delta"Cost"$, is mathematically equivalent to the ICER as a decision rule at a given $lambda$, with a positive value indicating the new strategy is worth adopting. Unlike the ICER, which is a ratio that can misbehave arithmetically when the incremental QALY denominator is small or changes sign, NMB is a simple linear combination with no such discontinuity, which is why it is commonly plotted on tornado diagrams instead of the ICER.], 25)
#glossary-entry("Network epidemic model", [A transmission model in which the population is represented as a graph of individuals (nodes) connected by contacts or partnerships (edges), with infection spreading only along existing edges rather than between any two randomly chosen individuals. This allows the model to represent real contact structures, such as household clusters or partnership networks, that a compartmental model collapses into a single average contact rate.], 22)
#glossary-entry("non-inferiority trial", [A trial design that, unlike a superiority trial, sets out to show that a new treatment is not meaningfully worse than an existing one, within a pre-agreed margin accepted in advance by the trial's designers and the relevant regulator as clinically unimportant. It is typically chosen when the new treatment offers some other advantage (cheaper, more convenient, fewer side effects), and because no finite trial can prove two treatments are exactly equal, its result is always "not worse by more than margin X," not "identical."], 4)
#glossary-entry("non-parametric bootstrap", [A resampling method that, unlike a parametric method which assumes the uncertain quantity follows a specific distributional form (Normal, Gamma, Beta) and estimates that distribution's parameters, makes no such assumption: it treats the observed sample as a stand-in for the population and estimates a sampling distribution purely by resampling the observed data itself, with replacement, repeatedly. It is a standard default for awkwardly skewed data, such as trial cost data, that does not fit a textbook distribution cleanly.], 25)
#glossary-entry("observer", [Code wrapped in `observeEvent({...})` or `observe({...})` that runs for its side effects, such as writing a log message, saving a file, or popping up a warning, rather than returning a value for display. Where a reactive expression is a formula that produces a value, an observer is an action that happens when something changes.], 29)
#glossary-entry("open-source license", [A legal document attached to a piece of code that grants others specific permissions, typically to view, use, modify, and redistribute it, that copyright law would otherwise withhold by default. Without an explicit license, code being publicly visible does not mean anyone has the legal right to reuse it.], 30)
#glossary-entry("Outcomes-based managed entry agreement", [An agreement that ties the price paid, or a refund, to evidence of how well a treatment performs after launch, rather than fixing a discount at launch regardless of subsequent performance. A coverage-with-evidence-development agreement is a conditional reimbursement decision in which the payer covers the drug now in exchange for a commitment to collect further population-level evidence (often via a mandatory registry) informing a formal re-appraisal, while a patient-level outcomes-based agreement ties payment or refund to an individual patient's own clinical response; patient-level agreements require clinical infrastructure most health systems lack at scale, which is why financial-based agreements remain far more common internationally despite outcomes-based agreements being more frequently discussed in the HTA literature.], 24)
#glossary-entry("parameterised report", [A single report template written so that certain inputs, such as a subgroup name, willingness-to-pay threshold, or country-specific unit costs, are left as variables rather than hard-coded, analogous to a SQL query with a placeholder that can be re-run for different values without rewriting the query. The report is rendered once per parameter set, producing a separate output file for each set while sharing identical structure and logic.], 28)
#glossary-entry("parametric survival curve (extrapolation)", [Extrapolation is projecting survival (and therefore costs and QALYs) beyond the finite period actually observed in a clinical trial, to cover a patient's whole remaining lifetime. A parametric survival curve achieves this by fitting a smooth mathematical formula, such as Weibull, log-normal, or log-logistic, to the observed trial data in place of the raw Kaplan-Meier step function, allowing the fitted curve to be evaluated at any time point, including years beyond the last observed patient; the resulting projection is only as trustworthy as the assumption that the fitted curve's shape continues to hold, which is why HTA agencies scrutinise the chosen parametric shape and demand sensitivity analyses across plausible alternatives.], 1)
#glossary-entry("penetration testing", [A deliberate, authorised attempt to find security weaknesses in a system by trying to break into it the way a malicious actor would, probing for ways to access restricted files, data, or functionality. For a web-hosted health-economic model, a practical lighter equivalent is checking that no server files are browsable, that authentication actually blocks unauthenticated access, and that input validation cannot be bypassed by unusual values, with a full formal penetration test usually reserved for applications handling real patient-level data.], 29)
#glossary-entry("permissive vs. copyleft license", [A permissive license (e.g. MIT, Apache 2.0, BSD) imposes very few conditions, allowing others to reuse, modify, and even sell derivative works, usually only requiring that the original copyright notice be kept. A copyleft license (the GPL family) imposes a stronger condition requiring that any derivative work incorporating the licensed code must itself be released under the same copyleft license, with the practical consequence that copyleft code cannot be quietly absorbed into a closed, proprietary product, while permissively-licensed code can.], 30)
#glossary-entry("pipeline / dependency graph", [A pipeline is the set of computational steps needed to go from raw inputs to final results, together with the fact that some steps depend on the outputs of others. Represented formally, this is a dependency graph, a map of which steps feed into which, which tools such as `targets` build explicitly from code rather than leaving implicit in the order scripts happen to be run.], 30)
#glossary-entry("positive/negative likelihood ratio", [Two numbers that translate a test's sensitivity and specificity into how much a result should shift suspicion of disease. The positive likelihood ratio indicates how many times more likely a positive result is in someone with the disease than without it, with higher values above 1 raising suspicion more strongly, while the negative likelihood ratio indicates how likely a negative result still is among diseased relative to non-diseased patients, with values closer to 0 providing stronger reassurance; unlike sensitivity/specificity, likelihood ratios can be combined directly with a patient's pre-test odds via Bayes' theorem to get post-test odds.], 11)
#glossary-entry("posterior distribution", [In Bayesian statistics, the updated belief about a parameter obtained by combining a prior (the belief before seeing current data, which may be vague or informed by previous studies) with the data actually observed, using the rules of probability. It is a full distribution of plausible values for the parameter, each with an associated probability, rather than a single number, and in realistic health-economic models it is usually too complex to compute an exact formula for, which is why samplers such as Stan and JAGS approximate it by drawing representative random samples rather than solving it analytically.], 2)
#glossary-entry("Poverty cases averted", [An ECEA financial-risk-protection metric counting whether health spending pushed a household's remaining income below an absolute poverty line that it would otherwise have stayed above, as distinct from catastrophic health expenditure, which measures whether spending crossed a fixed percentage-of-income threshold. A household can face catastrophic expenditure without being pushed into poverty, or be pushed into poverty by spending that is not catastrophic by the percentage convention, which is why poverty-cases-averted and catastrophic-health-expenditure-cases-averted are treated as complementary, not interchangeable, metrics, with a full ECEA reporting both where data allow.], 24)
#glossary-entry("prediction interval (vs. confidence interval)", [A confidence interval indicates how precisely the average true effect has been pinned down given the studies pooled, and narrows as more studies are added. A prediction interval instead answers what range a brand-new, not-yet-run study's true effect would plausibly fall in, and because individual studies' true effects genuinely vary (heterogeneity), it must be wider than the confidence interval, accounting for both uncertainty in the average and real study-to-study spread; quoting only the confidence interval when heterogeneity is substantial can make evidence look more consistent and generalisable than it really is.], 11)
#glossary-entry("QALY shortfall (absolute and proportional)", [Two measures of how much worse off, in QALY terms, someone with a given condition is compared to someone of the same age and sex without it. Absolute shortfall is the QALY count difference between general-population and condition-affected remaining QALY expectation, while proportional shortfall expresses that same gap as a fraction of the general population's expectation; the two measures can disagree about severity, for example because conditions affecting older patients may show a modest absolute but large proportional shortfall and vice versa for conditions affecting younger patients, which is why a decision rule may take whichever measure implies the higher weight rather than choosing one.], 24)
#glossary-entry("R package", [A standardised bundle of R code, documentation, and metadata that R can install, load, and check for internal consistency. Packaging one's own model this way means a single `library()` call loads every function in a known working state, instead of manually sourcing multiple files in the right order.], 30)
#glossary-entry("R6 class / R6-class objects", [R6 is one of R's systems for object-oriented programming, in which data and the functions (methods) that act on it are bundled together into a single object created from a class, a blueprint describing the fields and methods every object of that type will have. The syntax `Class$new(...)` creates a new object (like calling a constructor), and `object$method(...)` asks that object to run one of its own methods, as opposed to passing the object as a passive argument to a free-standing function.], 18)
#glossary-entry("radix sort / hash-based grouping", [Internal techniques for ordering or grouping rows of data quickly. A radix sort orders data by processing one digit, character, or byte at a time rather than repeatedly comparing whole values against each other, scaling better for integers, short strings, or factor-coded categories common in claims and registry data. Hash-based grouping computes a quick numeric "fingerprint" (hash) for each row's grouping value and uses it as a direct lookup key, finding matching rows almost instantly instead of scanning the whole table repeatedly; both are applied automatically by tools like `data.table` and explain why such tools become dramatically faster than alternatives at large scale.], 3)
#glossary-entry("random utility theory (McFadden)", [The statistical theory, named after economist Daniel McFadden, underlying discrete choice experiments and choice modelling generally, holding that each option a person chooses between has some fixed underlying attractiveness (utility) to that person plus a random, unpredictable wobble specific to each choice occasion. No single choice reveals much on its own, but across many choices made by many respondents the random wobbles average out, allowing statisticians to work backward from the pattern of choices to estimate how much each attribute contributes to overall attractiveness, which is why a DCE needs many repeated choice tasks per respondent rather than a single question.], 8)
#glossary-entry("reactive expression", [Code wrapped in `reactive({...})` that Shiny treats as a cached, auto-updating calculation, similar to a SQL view that recomputes only when the underlying data it depends on changes. It is called like a function, and Shiny only re-executes its body when one of the reactive values it reads has changed since the last call.], 29)
#glossary-entry("reactive programming", [A programming style in which relationships between values are declared rather than a sequence of steps being written. Unlike ordinary code, where a computed value becomes stale once an input changes until the line is re-run, a reactive expression is automatically re-run whenever the values it depends on change, similar to a spreadsheet formula cell that recalculates when an input cell is edited.], 29)
#glossary-entry("reference case", [An agency's defined standard for the base-case cost-effectiveness analysis: a fixed set of methodological choices (perspective, outcome measure, discount rate, time horizon) that every submission must follow unless explicitly justified otherwise. It exists so that models submitted by different companies for different diseases can be compared on a like-for-like basis by the decision-making committee; scenario and sensitivity analyses may deviate from it, but the base case cannot without justification.], 31)
#glossary-entry("Reference case (LMIC context)", [A standardised checklist of methodological choices, such as perspective, discount rate, uncertainty handling, and comparators, that a government or agency requires every economic evaluation submitted to it to follow, so that models built by different teams produce numbers that can be fairly compared against each other and against a common decision rule. NICE's methods guide is one such reference case; iDSI's is a reference case aimed at countries that do not yet have their own.], 31)
#glossary-entry("render", [To run every code chunk in a document, capture its output, and combine the result with the prose into a finished file (HTML, Word, PDF, etc.) via Pandoc -- conceptually equivalent to "compiling" a program, except the output is a formatted document rather than an executable. Triggered via `quarto render`, the RStudio/Positron "Render" button, or `quarto::quarto_render()`.], 28)
#glossary-entry("residual deviance / degrees of freedom", [Residual deviance is a single number summarising how far a GLM's fitted values are from the observed data (a generalisation of sum of squared errors; smaller is better). Degrees of freedom is roughly the number of observations minus the number of estimated parameters. For a well-fitting Poisson model these two values should be roughly equal, so their ratio should be close to 1; a ratio well above 1 signals overdispersion, which inflates Type I error. Both values are printed automatically by `summary()` on a fitted GLM in R.], 16)
#glossary-entry("Resource and its capacity", [In discrete event simulation (DES), a resource is any finite entity that competing entities must use (e.g. a nurse, scanner, or bed), and its capacity is the number of entities it can serve simultaneously. When capacity is exhausted, arriving entities wait in that resource's queue (finite or unlimited). Modelling resource capacity lets DES represent throughput limits -- such as nurse-hours, scanner-hours, or bed-days -- that a microsimulation cannot capture.], 22)
#glossary-entry("revealed preference", [A method of inferring how much people value something by observing the actual trade-offs they make in real decisions, rather than by directly asking them (stated preference). The classic example is the wage premium: comparing similar jobs that differ mainly in on-the-job fatality risk and observing the extra pay workers demand to accept the riskier job reveals an implicit valuation of safety. The approach generalises to any real-world choice where people visibly trade one thing for another, provided the choice is genuinely free and the trade-off can be isolated from confounding factors.], 4)
#glossary-entry("reverse proxy", [A server that sits in front of an application and forwards incoming requests to it, while handling tasks such as HTTPS encryption, load balancing across multiple copies of the app, and hiding the internal server address from the public internet.], 29)
#glossary-entry("rhino", [A newer, more opinionated framework from Posit/Appsilon for production Shiny apps that enforces a stricter folder structure, bundles linting and automated testing conventions, and integrates a JavaScript/Sass build pipeline so custom front-end styling and behaviour are first-class, version-controlled parts of the app rather than inline script calls scattered through UI code.], 29)
#glossary-entry("robust (sandwich-type) standard errors", [A method of calculating a parameter estimate's standard error that does not rely on the model's assumed error structure being exactly correct; the formula "sandwiches" a middle piece built from the observed data's actual variability between two outer pieces derived from the model. These are needed when inputs fed into a subsequent model (e.g. MAIC weights fed into a Cox model) are themselves estimated and carry their own uncertainty, which an ordinary standard error would ignore, producing an artificially narrow confidence interval.], 13)
#glossary-entry("roxygen2 / \@export tag", [`roxygen2` is the R tool that converts `#'`-prefixed comments above a function into formal help pages. The `\@export` tag tells it that the function should be listed in `NAMESPACE` as available to users of the package; functions without `\@export` remain internal helpers invisible outside the package. Omitting `\@export` on a function intended for users is a common cause of a function working when a script is run directly but not after packaging.], 30)
#glossary-entry("Royston-Parmar spline model", [A survival model that fits a flexible curve built from smoothly-joined piecewise segments (splines) to the log cumulative hazard over time, rather than assuming a single fixed parametric form (exponential, Weibull, etc.). The analyst chooses the number of "knots" (joints between curve segments), trading flexibility against the risk of over-fitting noise. It is particularly useful for extrapolation when observed survival data does not follow any single standard parametric shape.], 12)
#glossary-entry("S3 / S4 objects", [Two of R's object-oriented programming systems, which bundle data together with the methods that act on it. S3 is the older, lightweight system: a `class` attribute is attached to an ordinary R object, and functions named by convention (e.g. `print.my_model`) are dispatched automatically; there is no formal structure enforced, so mistakes can occur silently. S4 is stricter and more formal: a class's structure (required fields and their types) is declared explicitly in advance, and R checks that objects conform to that declaration. Model-parameter bundles, `survival` package model-fit objects, and several HEOR packages (`flexsurv`, `heemod`) use S3 or S4 objects internally.], 3)
#glossary-entry("Sargan (over-identification) test", [A test, possible only when more instruments are available than strictly needed for an instrumental-variables analysis (over-identification), of whether all instruments are internally consistent -- that is, whether every combination of them points to roughly the same causal effect. A statistically significant result warns that at least one instrument may be invalid, though it cannot identify which one.], 15)
#glossary-entry("scenario analysis vs. one-way/multi-way sensitivity analysis", [Both are deterministic (non-random) sensitivity analyses, but they serve different purposes. A scenario analysis varies a coherent, named bundle of simultaneous parameter changes motivated by a real-world narrative (e.g. an "optimistic" scenario altering several related parameters at once), used for stress-testing coherent alternative narratives. A one-way (or multi-way) sensitivity analysis instead varies one parameter (or two, jointly) at a time while holding others at base case, to isolate that parameter's individual influence on the result, typically reported as a tornado diagram or grid of ICERs.], 25)
#glossary-entry("Schoenfeld residuals", [A residual computed at each event time in survival data, capturing the gap between the covariate value of the patient who had the event and a weighted average of covariate values among everyone still at risk at that moment -- the model's implicit prediction under the proportional hazards assumption. If proportional hazards truly holds, these residuals should scatter randomly around zero over time; a systematic upward or downward drift indicates the covariate's effect on risk is not constant over time. `cox.zph()` provides a formal test and plot for this drift.], 10)
#glossary-entry("semantic versioning", [A convention for version numbers in the form MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH (e.g. `1.2.0`). PATCH increases for bug fixes that do not meaningfully change behaviour; MINOR increases when new functionality is added without breaking existing code; MAJOR increases when a change could break code written against an earlier version. It allows precise identification of which package version produced a given set of results and whether a later version is safe to re-run old analyses against.], 30)
#glossary-entry("Shiny module", [A self-contained pair of functions -- one for UI, one for server logic -- that bundles a piece of a Shiny app so it can be reused and tested like any other R function, rather than tangled into one monolithic `ui`/`server` pair. Modules are created with `moduleServer()` for the server half and a plain function returning UI elements for the UI half.], 29)
#glossary-entry("shinyapps.io", [A hosting service run by Posit that runs a Shiny app's files on Posit-managed servers and provides a public web address, requiring no knowledge of Linux server administration; users authenticate once and deploy with one R command.], 29)
#glossary-entry("Simpson's rule (half-cycle correction variant)", [A general numerical technique for estimating the area under a curve more accurately than a plain two-point average. The simplest half-cycle correction averages only the state occupancy at a cycle's start and end, approximating the true occupancy with a straight line between them. Simpson's rule improves on this by also using the occupancy at the cycle's midpoint and fitting a short parabolic segment through all three points, tracking a genuinely curving trend more closely, particularly when cycles are long or transition probabilities change quickly. In R-based Markov models this means sampling state-occupancy at an extra mid-cycle time point and combining it with a weighted-average formula.], 4)
#glossary-entry("single-arm trial", [A trial design that enrolls patients onto the investigational treatment only, with no concurrent randomized comparator group; effectiveness is assessed against an external benchmark (historical data, a different trial, or a modelled natural-history estimate) rather than against a within-trial control arm. Such designs are common in ultra-rare disease and some oncology indications where recruiting two arms is impractical, but they remove randomization's main statistical protection -- any observed difference from an external benchmark could reflect real treatment effect or could reflect differences between populations, time periods, or measurement methods.], 32)
#glossary-entry("Sobol' sequences / Quasi-Monte Carlo integration points", [A technique for numerically integrating over a covariate distribution by generating a large number of representative sample points covering the plausible range of covariate values, then averaging a model's prediction over them. Unlike ordinary Monte Carlo, which uses purely random points that cluster unevenly by chance, a Sobol' sequence generates points in a structured, non-random pattern that spreads them evenly across the space, giving a stable approximation even with a modest number of points. "Quasi-Monte Carlo" is the general name for this family of structured-point integration techniques, with Sobol' sequences the most common recipe for generating the points.], 13)
#glossary-entry("Software as a medical device (SaMD)", [Software intended for a medical purpose (diagnosis, prevention, monitoring, treatment, or management of a disease or condition) that performs that purpose without being embedded inside a physical hardware medical device -- the software itself, running on a general-purpose smartphone or server, is the medical device from a regulatory standpoint. SaMD typically must clear a separate regulatory bar (e.g. UKCA/CE marking, with higher-risk SaMD requiring deeper scrutiny) before an HTA body will consider reimbursement, meaning it travels through a different regulatory gateway than a drug before HTA evaluation becomes relevant.], 32)
#glossary-entry("SQL injection", [A security flaw arising when a program builds a database query by pasting untrusted text (commonly user input) directly into the query string instead of keeping the query's structure and user-supplied values separate. A maliciously or carelessly crafted input can cause the resulting query to select unintended rows or execute commands never intended by the original query, because the database cannot distinguish supplied data from query instructions once they are concatenated into one string. Parameterised queries avoid this by sending the query's fixed structure and user-supplied values to the database as two separate pieces, so any value is always treated as literal data rather than as part of the query's logic.], 5)
#glossary-entry("step-down allocation", [A hospital/programme cost-accounting technique for spreading a shared overhead cost (e.g. facility electricity, building maintenance, central programme management) across the departments or services that benefit from it, when no direct per-patient bill exists for that overhead. It proceeds in sequence: the department serving the most other departments while receiving services from none is allocated first, using a sensible driver (floor space, staff headcount, visit counts); its cost (including any share it just received) is then passed to the next department in sequence, and so on, until every shared cost has been pushed down to the clinical service lines that treat patients.], 9)
#glossary-entry("stochastic (first-order) uncertainty", [The randomness in what happens to one simulated patient or run even when every input parameter is held at a single, perfectly known value -- distinct from parameter uncertainty, which concerns how precisely the parameter values themselves are known. A cohort Markov model that moves a deterministic proportion of the cohort each cycle has essentially no first-order uncertainty, while an individual-level microsimulation re-run with identical parameters still yields a slightly different cost and QALY total purely from random patient-path noise. Assessing parameter uncertainty cleanly in a microsimulation requires enough replications or simulated patients per draw that stochastic noise is averaged out first, or the two sources of spread become tangled in the output.], 25)
#glossary-entry("structural uncertainty vs. parameter uncertainty", [Parameter uncertainty concerns how confidently the numeric inputs to a given model structure are known, and is quantified by probabilistic sensitivity analysis (PSA). Structural uncertainty concerns whether the model's shape or structure is even the right one -- e.g. whether failed treatment should route through a separate Fail state or be folded into an increased mortality risk. Two analysts with identical, perfectly precise parameter estimates can still disagree on structure; that disagreement cannot be resolved with more PSA draws and requires building and comparing genuinely different model structures.], 25)
#glossary-entry("synthetic data", [Data generated by an algorithm -- typically a statistical or machine-learning model fitted to real patient data -- designed to reproduce the statistical patterns (distributions, correlations, event rates) of the original dataset, without any row in the synthetic dataset corresponding to a real, identifiable patient.], 34)
#glossary-entry("tagged release", [In git, a tag is a permanent, human-readable label (e.g. `v1.0-submission`) attached to a specific commit, and a release packages that tagged commit as a stable, downloadable snapshot of source code (and often a compiled version), frozen at that exact point. A release does not change after the fact: subsequent commits to the main branch do not alter what it contains, unlike a link to the repository's default view, which always shows the current state of the main branch.], 30)
#glossary-entry("targets pipeline", [A workflow built with the `targets` R package, which tracks dependencies between steps of an analysis (e.g. load data, clean data, fit model, render report) and re-runs only the steps whose inputs have changed since the last run, skipping the rest. The workflow is declared as a sequence of named, interdependent steps in a `_targets.R` file and run via `targets::tar_make()`, analogous to a smart build system such as `make`.], 28)
#glossary-entry("threshold analysis", [An analysis that solves algebraically or numerically for the exact value of a parameter at which the ICER equals a specific target -- typically a willingness-to-pay threshold, but potentially any other benchmark. It differs from a one-way sensitivity analysis, which reports the ICER at several chosen values of a parameter across a range; the output of a threshold analysis is a single number with a precise interpretation (e.g. "at any price below X, this drug is cost-effective at our threshold") rather than a table or curve of ICERs.], 25)
#glossary-entry("time trade-off / standard gamble", [Two techniques for converting judgements about a health state into a utility value between 0 (dead) and 1 (full health) for use in QALY calculations, by eliciting the judgement through a concrete choice rather than an abstract rating. Time trade-off asks how many years of a fixed remaining lifespan in an impaired health state a respondent would give up to have the remaining years in full health instead (e.g. trading 2 of 10 years implies a utility of 0.8). Standard gamble instead offers a choice between the impaired state for certain versus a gamble with probability $p$ of full recovery and $(1-p)$ of immediate death; the probability $p$ at which the respondent is indifferent is taken as the utility value. Both are grounded in formal economic theory of choice under uncertainty or time preference, and underlie country-specific EQ-5D value sets.], 4)
#glossary-entry("Trajectory", [In discrete event simulation (e.g. the `simmer` package), the template path an entity follows through a model (e.g. arrive, queue for a resource, be served, leave), built once as an R object and sent down by every arriving entity. It is the DES equivalent of a state-transition diagram, except steps are tied to resources and durations rather than to annual transition probabilities.], 22)
#glossary-entry("two-way fixed effects (TWFE)", [A regression approach that gives every unit (e.g. district) its own intercept adjustment and every time period its own intercept adjustment simultaneously, so comparisons are made relative to each unit's own average level and each period's own average level rather than a single shared baseline. This controls for anything constant within a unit or within a time period without having to measure those confounders explicitly. When treatment rolls out to different units at different times (staggered adoption), this simple structure can implicitly compare already-treated units against each other as invalid controls, distorting the estimated effect -- motivating purpose-built staggered-adoption estimators.], 15)
#glossary-entry("Type I error", [A false positive: concluding there is a real effect or difference when, in the underlying truth, none exists and what was observed was random noise in the particular sample. The conventional threshold of $alpha = 0.05$ means accepting a 5% chance of this false alarm whenever a true null effect is tested; underestimated standard errors make p-values look smaller than they should, inflating the true false-positive rate well beyond the nominal 5%.], 16)
#glossary-entry("unit testing", [Writing small, automated pieces of code whose sole job is to check that another specific piece of code behaves as expected, run automatically every time the underlying code changes. `testthat` is the standard R package for writing these automated checks.], 27)
#glossary-entry("Value of a statistical life (VSL)", [An aggregated monetary figure representing the implied value of "one statistical death avoided" across a large group, derived from small individual willingness-to-pay amounts for small reductions in mortality risk -- not an attempt to price any individual, identified person's life. For example, if 100,000 people would each pay \$100 for a programme reducing each person's annual death risk by 1 in 100,000, the total willingness to pay (\$10 million) buys exactly one statistical death avoided, so the implied VSL is \$10 million. VSL estimates are built from stated-preference surveys or revealed-preference studies such as wage differentials for riskier jobs (the "hedonic wage" method).], 24)
#glossary-entry("Value of implementation (EVI)", [A value-of-information-adjacent measure answering "how much is it costing us that a decision we have already made correctly isn't yet being followed in practice?" -- as distinct from EVPI, EVPPI, EVSI, and ENBS, which answer "how much is it worth to know more before deciding?" Implementation-focused VOI concerns the gap between a guideline being issued and a patient actually receiving the recommended regimen, rather than parameter uncertainty or whether the decision itself might be wrong.], 26)
#glossary-entry("value-of-a-statistical-life estimate", [A population-level monetary figure representing how much a group of people are collectively willing to pay to reduce everyone's risk of death by a small amount, derived either from wage premiums workers demand for measurably riskier jobs (revealed preference) or from surveys asking directly how much people would pay for a small reduction in their own death risk (stated preference). For example, if 100,000 people are each willing to pay \$50 for a risk reduction expected to prevent one death across the group, the estimate for that context is \$5 million -- the aggregate amount a population puts toward preventing one anonymous, statistical death, not the value of any one identified person's life. It is the standard tool used by cost-benefit analysis to convert mortality risk reduction into a monetary figure.], 4)
#glossary-entry("variable importance / SHAP values", [Variable importance is a family of post-hoc measures used with models (e.g. random forests) that lack a single interpretable coefficient, typically computed by checking how much model accuracy drops when a covariate's values are randomly shuffled, or by measuring how much a variable reduces prediction error on average across all tree splits (e.g. `MeanDecreaseGini`/`MeanDecreaseAccuracy`). SHAP values (SHapley Additive exPlanations) are a more modern alternative, borrowed from cooperative game theory, that apportion credit for one specific patient's prediction across each covariate, giving a per-individual breakdown rather than a model-wide ranking.], 34)
#glossary-entry("Weighted sum versus weighted product", [A weighted sum combines criterion scores by multiplying each score by its weight and summing the results ($"score" = sum_i w_i dot s_i$), treating criteria as compensatory -- a low score on one can be offset by a high score on another in proportion to their weights. A weighted product instead multiplies each score raised to the power of its weight ($"score" = product_i s_i^(w_i)$), so a score of zero on any criterion drives the whole product to zero regardless of performance elsewhere, making it far less forgiving of a catastrophic single-criterion failure but inappropriate when criteria are meant to trade off freely. Most published health-sector MCDA applications, including EVIDEM, default to a weighted-sum-style additive combination.], 24)
#glossary-entry("welfare economics, demand theory, public finance", [Three parent disciplines health economics draws on. Welfare economics studies how to judge whether a policy change makes society better or worse off overall. Demand theory models how much of something people buy at different prices and how that changes with income or preferences. Public finance studies how governments raise money through taxation and allocate it across competing priorities. HEOR borrows selectively from all three but narrows its focus to whether one specific intervention is worth paying for compared to the alternative use of that money, rather than whole-budget allocation or price-responsiveness of demand.], 1)
#glossary-entry("Willingness-to-pay (WTP) approach", [An approach that asks, directly or indirectly, how much money a person would give up to obtain a health benefit or avoid a health harm, and uses that amount as the monetary value of the benefit, consistent with how economists value goods and services generally by the preferences of those who hold them. Stated-preference surveys used to elicit WTP are vulnerable to hypothetical bias, and WTP for health is mechanically constrained by ability to pay, so a poorer person's WTP for an identical health gain is capped by a smaller budget than a richer person's -- an income-related distortion in a different direction from the human-capital approach's wage distortion, meaning neither approach is equity-neutral.], 24)
#glossary-entry("Wilson interval (Wilson score interval)", [An alternative method for calculating a confidence interval around a proportion that, unlike the simple normal-approximation method (estimate plus or minus a multiple of the standard error, which assumes a symmetric bell-curve sampling distribution), stays within the valid 0-100% range and gives more trustworthy coverage when the proportion is close to 0% or 100% or the sample size is small. It is derived by inverting a hypothesis-testing statistic rather than assuming symmetry. An asymmetric confidence interval around a reported proportion, or a proportion near 0 or 1, suggests it was built with a Wilson-type method rather than the simple symmetric approximation.], 17)
#glossary-entry("Wu-Hausman test", [A test of whether endogeneity is a practically important problem for a given dataset, by comparing the plain OLS regression estimate against the instrumental-variables (2SLS) estimate of the same effect. If treatment is not endogenous the two estimates should be similar; a statistically significant result means they disagree by more than chance would explain, indicating the OLS estimate is likely biased by unmeasured confounding and that the (usually less precise) instrumental-variables estimate should be trusted instead.], 15)
#glossary-entry("YAML", [A plain-text format for writing structured settings -- key-value pairs, lists, nested options -- designed to be readable by humans as well as machines. In a Quarto or R Markdown document it appears as front matter at the top of the file, fenced by three dashes above and below, specifying the title, author, output format(s), and parameters before the report's content is read. Indentation in YAML is meaningful, and a stray extra space is a common source of cryptic rendering errors.], 28)
#glossary-entry("YAML workflow file", [A YAML file that tells GitHub Actions what sequence of steps to run (e.g. install R, install packages, run checks) and when to run them (on every push, only on the main branch, nightly, etc.), written once and rarely modified afterward.], 30)

#v(0.5em)
#heor-practice[
  A handful of terms carry different meanings in different parts of this book -- "cross-validation" means something structurally different in Chapter 16's machine-learning context than in Chapter 27's health-economic model-validation context, and "ML" is used both for Machine Learning (per this book's own abbreviations list) and, in Chapter 11's meta-analysis context, for the maximum-likelihood tau-squared estimator. Where a genuine naming collision like this exists, the entry below notes it explicitly -- always confirm which sense is meant from the surrounding chapter's context, not from the term alone.
]
